{"results":[{"body":"November 9, 2025 - “Ingesting food which has started to ferment, the raisins and fruit inside the buns, what it’s doing is creating mouth alcohol,” Mr Ridley said.","href":"https://breathalyser.com.au/media/hot-cross-bun-breathalyser-reading-claim-put-to-the-test","title":"Hot Cross Bun breathalyser reading claim put to the test"},{"body":"January 7, 2019 - Pastries such as hot cross buns would likely have trace amounts of alcohol in them, as yeast converts sugar into carbon dioxide and ethanol, the latter of which would be detected by a breathalyzer.","href":"https://www.snopes.com/fact-check/hot-cross-bun-breathalyzer/","title":"Can Eating Hot Cross Buns Trigger False Positive Breathalyzer Results? | Snopes.com"},{"body":"January 8, 2019 - Certain types of foods where alcohol is produced from the food or is already present in it ... \"If you're eating anything, many things, particularly if you still had some of the food in your mouth then you are going to get a false reading.\" The company which manufactures the breathalyser seen in the video, Alcolizer Technology, has produced a whitepaper about what can impact a breath test.","href":"https://www.abc.net.au/news/2019-01-08/can-hot-cross-buns-produce-false-breathalyser-test/10698438","title":"Nutritionist cold on warnings that hot cross buns produce false breathalyser reading - ABC News"},{"body":"April 17, 2024 - However, the device was used incorrectly in the demo; detecting alcohol in the mouth, not in the blood. The video, archived here in a tweet shared over 5,000 times, shows an officer first taking a breathalyser test giving a negative result. Then he eats a hot cross bun and takes the test again, giving a reading of 0.21mg...","href":"https://factcheck.afp.com/no-you-wont-get-arrested-eating-hot-cross-buns-easter-nor-can-you-use-them-excuse-drunk-driving","title":"No, you won’t get arrested for eating hot cross buns this Easter, nor can you use them as an excuse for drunk driving | Fact Check"},{"body":"July 12, 2025 - Hot cross buns contain yeast, raisins, and dough, and when combined at higher temperatures, they undergo fermentation, creating alcohol. This alcohol can be detected in the mouth by breathalysers, resulting in positive alcohol readings.","href":"https://cyalcohol.com/article/why-do-hot-cross-buns-give-an-alcohol-reading","title":"Hot Cross Buns: A Surprising Alcohol Reading | CyAlcohol"}]}